Stored Procedures

    .group SQL statements and business logic into a single, reusable unit that runs inside the DB
    .helps:
        .execute multiple SQL statements as one operation
        .accept input and returning output values
        .reduce repeated code in applications
        .enforce consistent business rules across systems 

    
Types
    .categorized based on their use case and functionality


    .system-stored 
        .predefined stored procedures provided by the SQL server for performing admin tasks such as DB management, troubleshooting 
        or system configuration 

    .user-defined 
        .created by users to perform specific business operations 
    
    .extended 
        .those that allow SQL server to execute external programs written in other languages 

    .CLR 
        .those written in .NET languages and executed in SQL server 



Benefits
    .performance optimization
    .security - limit direct access to tables and protect sensitive data 
    .code reusability 
    .reduced network traffic - multiple operations run in one call
    .easy maintenance - updating a procedure updates all its uses automatically




Real world uses 
    .order processing systems - automatically inserts new orders, updates stock, and generates new invoices
    .employee management system - calculates salaries, deducts taxes, and creates monthly payslips
    .data validation - checks data before inserting new records 
    .audit logs - tracks changes to sensitive data such as user roles, and permissions for security 



Best practices
    .keep it simple 
    .use error handling 
    .avoid cursors - use set-based queries where possibble
    .use parameters - for flexibility 
    